 -- Fedora Edition <fedora@example.com>  Thu, 26 Nov 2026 14:59:03 +0800

cubic-fedora (2026.12.20-fedora1) noble; urgency=low

  * Added manual selection of the kernel source on the Kernel page:
    - The kernel list can now be switched between the original ISO
      kernel and the kernel installed in the chroot (/boot).
    - The list can be refreshed after installing a new kernel in the
      chroot terminal, and each source remembers its own selection.
    - When a chroot kernel is selected, the initramfs is checked for
      the dmsquash-live module (if lsinitrd is available); generation
      stops with a dracut command if the module is missing.
  * Fixed the selected kernel not being used by the generated disk
    image (kernel list selection had no effect for Fedora disks):
    - The Generate page skipped the kernel copy entirely when the boot
      directory was not identified (the usual case for Fedora family
      disks), so the ISO kept booting the original kernel.
    - The Generate page now applies the selected kernel (vmlinuz and
      initramfs) to the boot loader files that the boot configurations
      actually reference (the "linux", "linuxefi", "kernel", "initrd",
      "initrdefi" directives and the isolatedinux "initrd=" token, plus
      the common loader locations), rewriting the files in place at the
      same paths. Because the paths are unchanged, the BIOS
      configurations and the configuration embedded in the EFI system
      partition image (images/efiboot.img) both boot the new kernel.
    - When the loader files of the layout can not be identified (for
      example, a unified kernel image), the original boot files are
      retained and the page reports that the selection is not applied.
    - A note (log) explains that an initramfs generated for an
      installed kernel inside the chroot may require the dmsquash-live
      module ("dracut --add dmsquash-live --force ...") to boot the
      live session.
  * README: documented the kernel selection behaviour, and added
    instructions to build the package as an .rpm (Fedora/openSUSE/RHEL)
    and as an Arch package (PKGBUILD), in addition to .deb.

 -- Fedora Edition <fedora@example.com>  Thu, 26 Nov 2026 14:52:22 +0800

cubic-fedora (2026.12.18-fedora1) noble; urgency=low

  * Added automatic correction of the live media boot parameters for
    the Fedora family, fixing the hang after startup ("systemd(1):
    Failed to set up ... Permission denied" leading to "Failed to
    allocate manager object."):
    - The customized Linux file system can not carry the SELinux
      extended attributes, when it is extracted and repacked on a
      Debian family host (Ubuntu), where SELinux is not enabled. The
      live media then boots with the security labels missing, and
      PID 1 can not label its runtime and device directories
      (hundreds of "Permission denied" messages), and the boot stops
      before the session starts.
    - The kernel command lines in all boot configurations of the
      custom disk (boot/, EFI/, isolinux/, images/) are corrected:
      • "enforcing=0" is added to every Linux command line that
        references the live root device ("root=live:"), unless the
        line already controls SELinux ("enforcing=" or "selinux=",
        which are kept);
      • "rd.live.check" is removed: the media checksum embedded in
        the original disk image can never pass for a customized
        disk image ("Media check failed! ... System will halt in
        12 hours").
    - Both operations are idempotent (regenerating the disk image
      does not duplicate the parameters), apply only to the Fedora
      family, and are best-effort (a failure does not stop the
      generation).
    - The media check MD5 implantation added in the previous version
      is retained.

 -- Fedora Edition <fedora@example.com>  Mon, 24 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +0000

cubic-fedora (2026.12.17-fedora1) noble; urgency=low

  * Completed the boot fix for Fedora family disks: regenerate the
    media check MD5 checksum after creating the disk image.
    - The default boot entry of Fedora live media is "Test this media"
      (rd.live.check). The check compares the disk image against the
      MD5 checksum area embedded in the ISO; after remastering, the
      content changed but the embedded checksum was not updated, so
      dracut reported "Media check failed" with a 12 hour halt.
    - The Generate page now calls implantisomd5 (from the isomd5sum
      package) right after the disk image is created (before the disk
      image size and the MD5 sum are reported), so the embedded
      checksum always matches the generated image.
    - Added isomd5sum to the package dependencies.
  * The label correction from the previous release continues to work:
    with this release the Fedora family disks are expected
    to boot completely into the live desktop.

 -- Fedora Edition <fedora@example.com>  Mon, 24 Nov 2026 15:10:00 +0000

cubic-fedora (2026.12.16-fedora1) noble; urgency=low

  * Replaced the boot configuration label rewriting with an automatic
    volume id correction (Fedora family):
    - The live label references also exist inside the configuration
      embedded in the EFI system partition image (images/efiboot.img),
      which can not be rewritten. Instead of modifying the boot
      configurations to match the volume id, the custom volume id is
      now automatically corrected to match the label actually
      referenced by the boot configurations (scanned from the copied
      files into the custom disk; the most frequent reference wins).
    - The correction runs at the start of the "Update the disk name and
      installer information" step, before the checksums and before
      xorriso writes the disk image (option -V), so the generated disk
      image always carries the label the original kernel command line
      expects.
    - For existing projects that stored an incorrect volume id (for
      example, an historical value with the appended version number),
      the correction self-heals the disk image on the next Generate
      run, without restoring any files.
    - The correction is best-effort (a failure does not stop the
      generation), is logged with the previous and corrected values,
      and is not applied to Debian family disks.

