 -- Fedora Edition <fedora@example.com>  Mon, 24 Nov 2026 11:30:00 +0000

cubic-fedora (2026.12.8-fedora1) noble; urgency=low

  * Supported the "tree" live image layout of Fedora 45 (the LiveOS
    image, although it may be named squashfs.img, is an EROFS or
    squashfs image that directly contains the root file system tree,
    with hundreds of thousands of files, and no embedded root file
    system image):
    - The EROFS branch now detects whether the image contains an
      embedded root file system image (nested layout), or the root
      file system tree itself (tree layout).
    - For the tree layout, the root file system tree is copied
      directly into the custom root directory; nothing is mounted and
      no mount information is saved. On the Generate page, the custom
      root directory is repacked with mksquashfs (with the standard
      volatile path exclusions), like the Debian family flow.
    - The squashfs branch already handles this layout using the same
      logic as the Debian family; the log message was updated to
      describe the layout instead of the family.
    - The compress-root command now creates the target directory if it
      does not exist (the "LiveOS" directory is excluded from the
      custom disk copy for Fedora family projects).
    - The extract-root command now creates the destination directory
      before invoking unsquashfs (which fails if the parent directory
      does not exist).
  * The extraction monitor interval was reduced from 10 to 3 seconds.
  * The real-time progress is now reported for every layout:
    • unsquashfs percent (squashfs images),
    • rsync percent (EROFS and raw root file system images),
    • the 3 second monitor (MB staged so far).

 -- Fedora Edition <fedora@example.com>  Mon, 24 Nov 2026 10:00:00 +0000

cubic-fedora (2026.12.7-fedora1) noble; urgency=low

  * Improved the visibility of the extraction progress, so that a slow
    target disk can be told apart from a stalled process:
    - The EROFS and raw root file system layouts now use rsync (with
      --info=progress2) instead of cp to copy the image into the
      staging directory, so the progress bar reports percentages
      (cp does not report any progress).
    - The squashfs extraction runs with a monitor that reports the
      amount of data staged so far (in MB) to the console every 10
      seconds. If the staged size keeps growing, the target disk is
      slow (for example a USB or SMR disk, or a network mount); if it
      stops growing, the process is genuinely stalled.
    - The compress-root copy mode also uses rsync, for the same
      reason.
    - The real-time process output reporting now excludes the in-place
      progress updates drawn by rsync.

 -- Fedora Edition <fedora@example.com>  Mon, 23 Nov 2026 22:00:00 +0000

cubic-fedora (2026.12.6-fedora1) noble; urgency=low

  * Fixed the extraction failure "Can't find a valid SQUASHFS
    superblock" on Fedora 45 disks:
    - The LiveOS image on newer Fedora releases is not a squashfs image
      (it may be an EROFS image, or a raw ext2/ext3/ext4 root file
      system image). The extract-root command now detects the file
      system type of the source image (using blkid, with a fallback to
      the file command), and automatically handles three layouts:
      • squashfs image with an embedded root file system image
        (extracted into the staging directory, as before);
      • EROFS image (mounted read-only, copied into the staging
        directory);
      • raw ext2/ext3/ext4 root file system image (copied into the
        staging directory).
    - For the raw root file system layout, the modified image is
      copied back to the target file on the Generate page (the file
      format is preserved), instead of being repacked with
      mksquashfs.
    - Sibling files inside the source image are preserved when
      repacking.
    - Unsupported or unknown image types now produce a clear error
      message (asking for the output of file and blkid), instead of
      the misleading "Debian family layout" unsquashfs failure.

